<Doc>
    <Code code={`abide
> watch chat/room id=onboarding`} />

    <p>The address is the handler's route without its leading <code>/</code> — its file's path
    under <code>src</code> — and the args are <code>name=value</code> words or one JSON
    object, as <code>call</code> takes them. Against a deployed app it is a command of its own:
    <code>abide watch chat/room id=onboarding --url https://app.example</code>.</p>

    <Section title="The channel is the same channel">
        <p>There is nothing terminal-shaped about this. <code>watch</code> asks the <code>GET</code>
        for the event stream any client may ask for (18.20), and that stream carries what the page's
        mux carries, frame for frame. So the array is the same array and the messages are the same
        messages. What you see in the shell is what the page sees, and a discrepancy is a real one
        rather than a difference between two clients.</p>

        <p>That is the payoff of one follow for every client. A debugging tool that speaks a
        different protocol is a tool whose agreement with the app has to be believed.</p>

        <p>A <code>GET</code> not answered from a <code>global</code> value has nothing to follow,
        so it answers once and <code>watch</code> prints it once.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/keep-a-room-of-callers-in-sync')}>Live updates</a> ·
        <a href={url('/values/let-anything-publish-and-anything-read')}>Channels</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The value first, then its patches">
        <p>A follow is sent the value with its version, then what each patch wrote as JSON Patch
        (18.6, 18.11), which is why joining midway through a conversation shows you the
        conversation. It is one mechanism for three handoffs — seeding a first render, a connection
        that dropped and reopened, and a shell that just connected.</p>

        <p>A reconnect names the version it last held (18.9). Where that is still the value's
        version nothing is resent; anywhere else, a restarted process included, the value is sent
        whole, because a version names one production or patch in no other process (18.7).</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The shell watches, and does not write">
        <p><code>watch</code> only reads. The way into a channel from outside the process is a
        <code>POST</code> whose handler patches it, so to change a room from a terminal,
        <code>call</code> that handler as a page would. Its rungs decide whether the shell may, the
        same as for any caller (16.11).</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/keep-a-room-of-callers-in-sync')}>Live updates</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a></p>
    </Section>

